# Cursor rules for Coffee POS
# Same rules as CLAUDE.md (the fuller version). Keep the two files in step when either changes.

## Project
Coffee POS: order taking for one small coffee shop in the Philippines. One computer runs `node server.js`; phones, tablets and laptops on the same Wi-Fi use it in a browser. Pages: order screen (`/`, no PIN), Products & prices with recipes and AI settings (`/admin`, PIN), Sales reports with order voiding and optional AI summary/questions (`/reports`, PIN). Users are non-technical staff and the owner.

## Stack
- Node.js 22.13+, zero npm dependencies, no build step. Built-ins only: `node:http`, `node:sqlite` (`DatabaseSync`), global `fetch`.
- Frontend: plain HTML pages in `public/` with inline `<script>` blocks, shared `common.js`, `recipes.js`, `app.css`. No framework, no bundler.
- Run: `node server.js` (or `npm start`, or `start-windows.bat`). Env: `PORT` (3000), `ADMIN_PIN` (1234), `TZ` (Asia/Manila), `DB_FILE` (data/coffee.db), `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `AI_PROVIDER` (openai|anthropic|off), `AI_MODEL`.
- Test against a throwaway DB: `DB_FILE=/tmp/test.db PORT=3001 node server.js`. No test suite or linter; verify by running the app or curling the API with `X-Admin-Pin`.

## Files
- `server.js`: server, schema and seed, products, orders, reports, AI routes, static files.
- `recipes.js` / `recipes-seed.js`: recipe table, routes, starter recipes (placeholders). Hooked in via `setupRecipes(db, helpers)`.
- `ai.js`: optional AI (OpenAI default `gpt-5-mini`, Claude default `claude-haiku-5-5`), built-in fetch, prompts.
- `public/index.html` order screen; `public/admin.html` products, recipes, AI settings; `public/reports.html` reports, orders, CSV/print, AI.
- `public/common.js` helpers: `api()`, `peso()`, `esc()`, `$()`, `toast()`, `header()`, `requirePin()`.

## Data (data/coffee.db, SQLite, WAL, foreign keys on)
- `products`, `prices` (temp Hot/Cold x size Small/Medium/Large; missing row = size not sold).
- `orders`: `created_at` is local-time text `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`; reports group by prefix. `status` is `paid` or `void`.
- `order_items`: `product_name`, `category`, `unit_price`, `line_total` are snapshots at time of sale.
- `recipes` (JSON ingredients/steps per product and temp), `settings` (`ai_*` keys, including API keys).
- Schema changes must be additive and safe on an existing shop database. Never drop or rewrite sales data.

## API
- Open: `GET /api/products`, `POST /api/orders`, `GET /api/recipes/:id`.
- All other routes need the `X-Admin-Pin` header.
- Throw `HttpError(status, message)`; messages are shown to staff, so use plain English that says what to do.

## Rules (must follow)
1. The order screen never depends on AI. `index.html`, `GET /api/products` and `POST /api/orders` never call `ai.js` or any outside service and work the same with no key, no internet, or a failing provider. AI is admin-only and optional.
2. Prices are saved on each order. `createOrder` reads the current DB price at sale time and stores it on the line. Never compute past totals from the current `prices` table, never trust browser-sent prices, keep the `expectedTotal` check.
3. Works offline on a shop LAN. No CDNs, web fonts, external scripts or images, analytics, or remote calls outside the optional AI assistant.
4. No new dependencies, frameworks or build steps without the owner's explicit approval.
5. No secrets or data in git: never commit `data/`, `*.db` files, API keys or a real admin PIN. Keep `.gitignore` excluding them. API keys stay in `settings` or env vars and are never sent to the browser (only `maskKey()` hints).
6. Customer names never go to the AI. Keep `summaryData()` and `askContext()` to aggregated totals, product names, prices and quantities.
7. Money is pesos: round with `money()` on the server, display with `peso()` as ₱1,250.00. Validate quantities and amounts on the server.
8. Voided orders stay in the DB with `status = 'void'` and are excluded from all reports and AI figures.
9. Escape user text with `esc()` before inserting into HTML.
10. Keep the UI simple for staff: large touch targets, plain wording, fast on a phone.

## Conventions
- CommonJS, 2-space indent, single quotes, semicolons; match surrounding style.
- Prepared statements with `?` params for all SQL; multi-row writes in BEGIN/COMMIT with ROLLBACK on error.
- Grow features as separate modules with a small hook into `server.js`, like `recipes.js` and `ai.js`.
- Saved admin AI settings win over environment variables.
- Update `README.md` (written for the non-technical owner) when behaviour, settings or files change.
